# 5. Analytical Method

The analysis uses descriptive statistics and subgroup comparisons.

### Overall Readmission Rate


Readmission Rate =

(Number of Readmitted Records / Total Records) × 100
Subgroup Readmission Rate =
(Readmitted Records in Subgroup / Total Records in Subgroup) × 100

# 6. Verified Metric 1 – Overall Readmission Rate

There were 3 readmitted records out of 10 total records.

(3 / 10) × 100 = 30%
Result

Overall readmission rate = 30%.

This serves as the baseline readmission measure for the supplied sample.

#7. Verified Metric 2 – Readmission Among Heart Disease Records

The dataset contains two records with a diagnosis of heart disease:

Patient Record	Readmitted
PAT-203	Yes
PAT-209	Yes

Therefore:

(2 / 2) × 100 = 100%
Result

Heart disease subgroup readmission rate = 100% in this sample.

Interpretation

Both heart disease records in the supplied sample were marked as readmitted.

However, this subgroup contains only 2 records.

Therefore, the result should be interpreted as a descriptive sample-level observation, not as a population-level clinical rate.

It also does not demonstrate that heart disease caused readmission.

# 8. Verified Metric 3 – Medication Pattern Among Readmitted Records

The three readmitted records had the following primary medications:

Primary Medication	Readmitted Records
Atorvastatin	1
Ibuprofen	1
Clopidogrel	1
Result

The three readmitted records involved three different primary medications.
There is therefore no single primary medication that accounts for all readmissions in this sample.

Interpretation

The supplied data do not support a conclusion that one particular medication caused or was responsible for readmission.

Medication exposure should therefore be treated as a descriptive variable rather than a causal explanation.

